  {
   "id": "course-map",
   "scene": "CourseMapSequence",
   "section": 1,
   "title": "From $y' = ay + q$ to partial differential equations",
   "summary": "The series climbs from one first order equation to second order equations, systems with matrices, numerical methods and PDEs.",
   "initial": {
    "title": "The model equation",
    "tex": "\\frac{dy}{dt} = a\\,y + q(t)",
    "explanation": "The rate of change of $y$ is a growth term $ay$ plus an outside input $q(t)$. Linear: $y$ appears only to the first power.",
    "frame": "animations/course-map/state-00.jpg"
   },
   "steps": [
    {
     "index": 1,
     "title": "Nonlinear and second order",
     "relation": "classify",
     "relationLabel": "Classification",
     "tex": "y' = f(y), \\qquad m y'' + b y' + k y = f(t)",
     "explanation": "A right side such as $y^2$ makes the equation nonlinear. Newton's law brings second derivatives: mass, damping, spring, force.",
     "initialFrame": "animations/course-map/state-00.jpg",
     "finalFrame": "animations/course-map/state-01.jpg",
     "transition": {
      "src": "animations/course-map/step-01.mp4",
      "duration": 2.4,
      "frames": 72,
      "masterFrames": [
       8,
       80
      ],
      "bytes": 47818,
      "codec": "h264"
     }
    },
    {
     "index": 2,
     "title": "Systems: vectors and matrices",
     "relation": "model",
     "relationLabel": "Modelling step",
     "tex": "\\mathbf{y}' = A\\,\\mathbf{y}, \\qquad \\mathbf{y}'' + S\\,\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{0}",
     "explanation": "Several coupled unknowns form a vector. Eigenvectors of $A$ uncouple the system into separate first order equations.",
     "initialFrame": "animations/course-map/state-01.jpg",
     "finalFrame": "animations/course-map/state-02.jpg",
     "transition": {
      "src": "animations/course-map/step-02.mp4",
      "duration": 1.7333,
      "frames": 52,
      "masterFrames": [
       79,
       131
      ],
      "bytes": 51440,
      "codec": "h264"
     }
    },
    {
     "index": 3,
     "title": "Numbers and PDEs",
     "relation": "recap",
     "relationLabel": "Recap",
     "tex": "\\text{Euler} \\to \\text{ode45}, \\qquad u_t = u_{xx}, \\quad u_{tt} = u_{xx}",
     "explanation": "Most real equations are solved numerically, from Euler's step to ode45. The series ends with the heat and wave equations.",
     "initialFrame": "animations/course-map/state-02.jpg",
     "finalFrame": "animations/course-map/state-03.jpg",
     "transition": {
      "src": "animations/course-map/step-03.mp4",
      "duration": 2.3667,
      "frames": 71,
      "masterFrames": [
       130,
       201
      ],
      "bytes": 77017,
      "codec": "h264"
     }
    }
   ],
   "totalDuration": 6.5,
   "masterFrames": 201,
   "renderSeconds": 7.3
  },
  {
   "id": "calculus-check",
   "scene": "CalculusCheckSequence",
   "section": 2,
   "title": "Checking $y = \\int_0^t e^{t-s}q(s)\\,ds$ with the product rule",
   "summary": "Pull $e^t$ out of the integral, differentiate the product, and use the fundamental theorem: the two terms of the product rule are the two terms of $y' = y + q$.",
   "initial": {
    "title": "A formula to check",
    "tex": "y(t) = \\int_0^t e^{t-s}\\,q(s)\\,ds",
    "explanation": "Claim: this solves $y' = y + q(t)$ with $y(0) = 0$. Each input $q(s)$ grows by $e^{t-s}$.",
    "frame": "animations/calculus-check/state-00.jpg"
   },
   "steps": [
    {
     "index": 1,
     "title": "Split the exponential",
     "relation": "identity",
     "relationLabel": "Identity (=)",
     "tex": "y(t) = e^{t}\\int_0^t e^{-s}\\,q(s)\\,ds",
     "explanation": "$e^{t-s} = e^t e^{-s}$, and $e^t$ does not depend on $s$, so it comes outside: $y$ is a product.",
     "initialFrame": "animations/calculus-check/state-00.jpg",
     "finalFrame": "animations/calculus-check/state-01.jpg",
     "transition": {
      "src": "animations/calculus-check/step-01.mp4",
      "duration": 3.3667,
      "frames": 101,
      "masterFrames": [
       8,
       109
      ],
      "bytes": 74199,
      "codec": "h264"
     }
    },
    {
     "index": 2,
     "title": "Product rule",
     "relation": "compute",
     "relationLabel": "Computation",
     "tex": "y' = e^{t}\\int_0^t e^{-s}q(s)\\,ds + e^{t}\\cdot e^{-t}q(t)",
     "explanation": "Derivative of $e^t$ times the integral, plus $e^t$ times the derivative of the integral. The fundamental theorem gives that derivative: $e^{-t}q(t)$.",
     "initialFrame": "animations/calculus-check/state-01.jpg",
     "finalFrame": "animations/calculus-check/state-02.jpg",
     "transition": {
      "src": "animations/calculus-check/step-02.mp4",
      "duration": 4.1,
      "frames": 123,
      "masterFrames": [
       108,
       231
      ],
      "bytes": 159407,
      "codec": "h264"
     }
    },
    {
     "index": 3,
     "title": "Recognise both terms",
     "relation": "implies",
     "relationLabel": "Consequence (⇒)",
     "tex": "y' = y + q(t)",
     "explanation": "The first term is $y$ itself, and $e^te^{-t} = 1$ leaves $q(t)$. The equation is satisfied.",
     "initialFrame": "animations/calculus-check/state-02.jpg",
     "finalFrame": "animations/calculus-check/state-03.jpg",
     "transition": {
      "src": "animations/calculus-check/step-03.mp4",
      "duration": 3.6333,
      "frames": 109,
      "masterFrames": [
       230,
       339
      ],
      "bytes": 110112,
      "codec": "h264"
     }
    }
   ],
   "totalDuration": 11.1,
   "masterFrames": 339,
   "renderSeconds": 8.4
  },
  {
   "id": "tangent-taylor",
   "scene": "TangentTaylorSequence",
   "section": 2,
   "title": "Tangent line, tangent parabola, Taylor series",
   "summary": "Approximate $e^t$ near $t = 0$: the line uses the slope, the parabola adds bending, and all derivatives together give the exact series.",
   "initial": {
    "title": "The curve $e^t$",
    "tex": "f(t) = e^{t},\\qquad f(0) = f'(0) = f''(0) = 1",
    "explanation": "Every derivative of $e^t$ is $e^t$, so all of them equal $1$ at $t = 0$.",
    "frame": "animations/tangent-taylor/state-00.jpg"
   },
   "steps": [
    {
     "index": 1,
     "title": "Tangent line",
     "relation": "define",
     "relationLabel": "Definition / choice",
     "tex": "f(\\Delta t) \\approx f(0) + \\Delta t\\, f'(0) = 1 + \\Delta t",
     "explanation": "Start at the height and follow the slope. Good for small steps; the curve pulls away for large ones.",
     "initialFrame": "animations/tangent-taylor/state-00.jpg",
     "finalFrame": "animations/tangent-taylor/state-01.jpg",
     "transition": {
      "src": "animations/tangent-taylor/step-01.mp4",
      "duration": 2.5667,
      "frames": 77,
      "masterFrames": [
       8,
       85
      ],
      "bytes": 37732,
      "codec": "h264"
     }
    },
    {
     "index": 2,
     "title": "Tangent parabola",
     "relation": "compute",
     "relationLabel": "Computation",
     "tex": "f(\\Delta t) \\approx 1 + \\Delta t + \\tfrac{1}{2}(\\Delta t)^2",
     "explanation": "Add $\\tfrac12(\\Delta t)^2 f''(0)$ to account for the bending. Much closer.",
     "initialFrame": "animations/tangent-taylor/state-01.jpg",
     "finalFrame": "animations/tangent-taylor/state-02.jpg",
     "transition": {
      "src": "animations/tangent-taylor/step-02.mp4",
      "duration": 2.9333,
      "frames": 88,
      "masterFrames": [
       84,
       172
      ],
      "bytes": 73189,
      "codec": "h264"
     }
    },
    {
     "index": 3,
     "title": "All derivatives: the Taylor series",
     "relation": "implies",
     "relationLabel": "Consequence (⇒)",
     "tex": "e^{t} = 1 + t + \\frac{t^2}{2!} + \\frac{t^3}{3!} + \\cdots",
     "explanation": "The $n$-th term is $t^n f^{(n)}(0)/n!$. For $e^t$ the infinite sum equals the function exactly.",
     "initialFrame": "animations/tangent-taylor/state-02.jpg",
     "finalFrame": "animations/tangent-taylor/state-03.jpg",
     "transition": {
      "src": "animations/tangent-taylor/step-03.mp4",
      "duration": 3.3667,
      "frames": 101,
      "masterFrames": [
       171,
       272
      ],
      "bytes": 66722,
      "codec": "h264"
     }
    }
   ],
   "totalDuration": 8.867,
   "masterFrames": 272,
   "renderSeconds": 8.5
  },
  {
   "id": "exponential-response",
   "scene": "ExponentialResponseSequence",
   "section": 3,
   "title": "The exponential response $Y = 1/(s-a)$",
   "summary": "Substitute $Ye^{st}$, divide out $e^{st}$, solve for $Y$, then add the null solution and match $y(0)$.",
   "initial": {
    "title": "Exponential input",
    "tex": "y' = a\\,y + e^{st}",
    "explanation": "The best input: its derivative is itself times $s$.",
    "frame": "animations/exponential-response/state-00.jpg"
   },
   "steps": [
    {
     "index": 1,
     "title": "Substitute $y = Ye^{st}$",
     "relation": "substitute",
     "relationLabel": "Substitution",
     "tex": "s\\,Y e^{st} = a\\,Y e^{st} + e^{st}",
     "explanation": "The derivative brings down $s$. Every term carries $e^{st}$.",
     "initialFrame": "animations/exponential-response/state-00.jpg",
     "finalFrame": "animations/exponential-response/state-01.jpg",
     "transition": {
      "src": "animations/exponential-response/step-01.mp4",
      "duration": 2.7333,
      "frames": 82,
      "masterFrames": [
       8,
       90
      ],
      "bytes": 76620,
      "codec": "h264"
     }
    },
    {
     "index": 2,
     "title": "Divide by $e^{st}$",
     "relation": "solve",
     "relationLabel": "Solve",
     "tex": "(s - a)\\,Y = 1 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; Y = \\frac{1}{s - a}",
     "explanation": "$e^{st}$ is never zero, so divide it out and collect the $Y$ terms.",
     "initialFrame": "animations/exponential-response/state-01.jpg",
     "finalFrame": "animations/exponential-response/state-02.jpg",
     "transition": {
      "src": "animations/exponential-response/step-02.mp4",
      "duration": 3.6,
      "frames": 108,
      "masterFrames": [
       89,
       197
      ],
      "bytes": 85767,
      "codec": "h264"
     }
    },
    {
     "index": 3,
     "title": "Add a null solution",
     "relation": "model",
     "relationLabel": "Modelling step",
     "tex": "y = \\frac{e^{st}}{s-a} + C e^{at}",
     "explanation": "$Ce^{at}$ solves $y' = ay$, so adding it keeps the equation true and gives freedom to match $y(0)$.",
     "initialFrame": "animations/exponential-response/state-02.jpg",
     "finalFrame": "animations/exponential-response/state-03.jpg",
     "transition": {
      "src": "animations/exponential-response/step-03.mp4",
      "duration": 2.5667,
      "frames": 77,
      "masterFrames": [
       196,
       273
      ],
      "bytes": 87673,
      "codec": "h264"
     }
    },
    {
     "index": 4,
     "title": "Match $y(0)$ and regroup",
     "relation": "solve",
     "relationLabel": "Solve",
     "tex": "y = y(0)\\,e^{at} + \\frac{e^{st} - e^{at}}{s - a}",
     "explanation": "$C = y(0) - 1/(s-a)$. Regrouped: the starting value grows, and the input part starts from $0$.",
     "initialFrame": "animations/exponential-response/state-03.jpg",
     "finalFrame": "animations/exponential-response/state-04.jpg",
     "transition": {
      "src": "animations/exponential-response/step-04.mp4",
      "duration": 2.9667,
      "frames": 89,
      "masterFrames": [
       272,
       361
      ],
      "bytes": 98407,
      "codec": "h264"
     }
    }
   ],
   "totalDuration": 11.867,
   "masterFrames": 361,
   "renderSeconds": 10.5
  },
  {
   "id": "resonance",
   "scene": "ResonanceSequence",
   "section": 3,
   "title": "Resonance: $0/0$ becomes $t\\,e^{at}$",
   "summary": "When $s = a$ the input part is $0/0$. L'Hôpital's rule in $s$ gives the resonant solution $t\\,e^{at}$.",
   "initial": {
    "title": "The input part",
    "tex": "y_{vp} = \\frac{e^{st} - e^{at}}{s - a}",
    "explanation": "Fine whenever $s \\ne a$.",
    "frame": "animations/resonance/state-00.jpg"
   },
   "steps": [
    {
     "index": 1,
     "title": "Let $s \\to a$",
     "relation": "caution",
     "relationLabel": "Caveat",
     "tex": "\\frac{e^{at} - e^{at}}{a - a} = \\frac{0}{0}",
     "explanation": "Top and bottom both vanish. Not infinite: undecided, so look closer.",
     "initialFrame": "animations/resonance/state-00.jpg",
     "finalFrame": "animations/resonance/state-01.jpg",
     "transition": {
      "src": "animations/resonance/step-01.mp4",
      "duration": 2.8667,
      "frames": 86,
      "masterFrames": [
       8,
       94
      ],
      "bytes": 60299,
      "codec": "h264"
     }
    },
    {
     "index": 2,
     "title": "L'Hôpital: differentiate in $s$",
     "relation": "compute",
     "relationLabel": "Computation",
     "tex": "\\frac{\\partial_s\\left(e^{st} - e^{at}\\right)}{\\partial_s (s - a)} = \\frac{t\\,e^{st}}{1}",
     "explanation": "The derivative of $e^{st}$ with respect to $s$ brings down $t$; $e^{at}$ does not depend on $s$.",
     "initialFrame": "animations/resonance/state-01.jpg",
     "finalFrame": "animations/resonance/state-02.jpg",
     "transition": {
      "src": "animations/resonance/step-02.mp4",
      "duration": 3.0667,
      "frames": 92,
      "masterFrames": [
       93,
       185
      ],
      "bytes": 56536,
      "codec": "h264"
     }
    },
    {
     "index": 3,
     "title": "The resonant response",
     "relation": "implies",
     "relationLabel": "Consequence (⇒)",
     "tex": "y_{vp} = t\\,e^{at}",
     "explanation": "Set $s = a$. The factor $t$ is the signature of resonance: the response outgrows $e^{at}$.",
     "initialFrame": "animations/resonance/state-02.jpg",
     "finalFrame": "animations/resonance/state-03.jpg",
     "transition": {
      "src": "animations/resonance/step-03.mp4",
      "duration": 3.3,
      "frames": 99,
      "masterFrames": [
       184,
       283
      ],
      "bytes": 70980,
      "codec": "h264"
     }
    }
   ],
